  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThe last of the rain was slowly falling apart and moving northeast \nas a subtle shortwave trough embedded within an active southern \nbranch of the polar jet continued to push northeast across southern \nKansas. A weak cold front moved south yesterday and reached a line \nfrom just north of Elkhart-Hugoton to around Dodge City to near \nSaint John at 06Z (1 AM CDT). This front will eventually become a \nwarm front later this morning as warm/moist southerly winds advance \nnorthward in response to a stronger upstream polar jet streak moving \nacross the Northern Rockies.\n\nAs the frontal zone advances slowly north through this morning, \nareas of fog will develop in favorable easterly upslope winds \nalong/just north of the front. We have added in areas of fog in the \nWx grids, and even though we have not issued a headline (yet) for \ndense fog, it is something the overnight shift will need to monitor \nclosely for possible widespread dense fog in the roughly 5 AM to 11 \nAM time frame. Regardless of fog development or not,d low stratus \ncloud will be widespread and very difficult to scatter out much of \nthe day Tuesday. This will have an impact on temperatures, and we \nhave lowered these as well toward the latest 00Z REFS 50th \npercentile temperatures. Trego and Ellis County will struggle to get \nabove the mid to upper 50s in the afternoon, so the official \nforecast up along I-70 may still be a bit too warm. That being said, \nthe air mass south of the front advancing northward is quite warm \nand moist with dewpoints in the upper 50s to around 60F, which is \nrather anomalous for 27 October. \n\nOn Wednesday, with west-southwest flow aloft, southwest Kansas will \nbe well into the warm/moist air mass, and this will be reflected at \nthe surface with afternoon temperatures well into the mid to upper \n70s. As the Northern Rockies storm system(s) push east across the \nDakotas, southwest flow aloft will continue across western Kansas, \nand this will keep the warm/moist air mass across our region as the \nnext rather substantial southern stream/subtropical Pacific jet \nenters northern Mexico and eventually Southwest and West Texas by \nthe time we get to late Friday into the early weekend. A shortwave \ntrough will emerge in the left-exit region of the extended \nsubtropical jet over West Texas and lift north-northeast toward \nsouthern Kansas by late Friday-Friday Evening. This subtropical \ndisturbance will also have unabated access to anomalously high gulf \nmoisture which will advance northward and support a fairly robust \nrainfall event, again, across Southern into portions of Central \nPlains. It will all come down to storm track for southwest Kansas, \nwith heavier precipitation favored more across West Texas, pushing \ninto the Panhandles and adjacent southwest Kansas generally south of \nthe Arkansas River. The latest 01Z NBM does have 0.50\" prob of \nexceedance in the 50-65% range for this Friday into early Saturday \nsystem.\n\nGoing beyond into late weekend/early next week, more and more global \nmodel ensemble members are suggesting a constructive phasing of the \nnorthern polar jet stream with the active subtropical jet into a \nlarge mid-latitude cyclone that would be capable of widespread \nmoderate/heavy rain and thunderstorm activity. These are all just \nearly signals at this point, and the focus initially will be on fine-\ntuning the rainfall forecast for Friday-Saturday.",
